					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tujwKsS95OEAtB1qa",
					"name": "Enhanced Move (Ground)",
					"reference": "B52",
					"local_notes": "Top sprinting speed x2 (Move 14 sprints at 28 yd/s, about 90 km/h). Combat Move unchanged.\n\nLore: Custodians move with a speed that stuns even Space Marines who fight beside them; Chris Wraight's Custodes novels describe them crossing distances in moments that should take a squad minutes.\n\nDesign: Enhanced Move (Ground) 1 (B52, 20 points) at the user's direction; a full level against the Astartes' half level keeps Custodes clearly the faster of the two.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"points_per_level": 20,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1
				},
				{
					"id": "tKPUoIz7SGr7xKsC2",
					"name": "Unfazeable",
					"reference": "B99",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Custodian has stood in the Sanctum Imperialis for ten thousand years, fought Horus's own Sons of Horus, boarded daemon-infested hulks, and duelled things with no right to exist. Black Library and Codex background never show a Custodian rattled, hesitant, or surprised by anything short of the direct will of the Emperor.\n\nDesign: Unfazeable (B99, 15 points): full immunity to Fear and to being surprised, stronger than the Fearlessness the Astartes package uses, matching the framework's flat \"immune to fear\" instruction rather than a leveled resistance.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Imperium",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"base_points": 15
				},
				{
					"id": "tss19UKUnVDnpGVTk",
					"name": "Unaging",
					"reference": "B17",
					"local_notes": "Lore: The Ten Thousand have served since before the Imperium existed; named Custodians such as Valerian, Aleya, and the long-lived characters of Wraight's novels are treated as ancient by any human standard without any hint of physical decline. The exact biological mechanism (whether it is a one-off transformation or an ongoing process) is not detailed in the fiction consulted for this build.\n\nDesign: Unaging (B17, 15 points): no HT penalties or death checks from old age, matching \"lives for millennia\" without claiming a specific immortality mechanism the sources don't describe.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Imperium",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"base_points": 15
				},
				{
					"id": "tVYAUaLTcKZBxcvTw",
					"name": "Combat Reflexes",
					"reference": "B43",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Centuries of drilling against the finest opponents the Imperium can offer (including each other) leave a Custodian essentially unshakeable in combat, never fumbling a reaction to a sudden threat.\n\nDesign: Combat Reflexes (B43, 15 points), as the Astartes package.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Imperium",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"base_points": 15
				},
				{
					"id": "t6d1vlSZxXQTqv2cf",
					"name": "Custodian Senses",
					"reference": "B16",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Codex background credits Custodians with reflexes and situational awareness that let them track and parry attacks other elites simply cannot perceive in time; this is modelled as raw Perception rather than the itemised acute-sense organs the Astartes package uses, since no source breaks a Custodian's senses down organ by organ.\n\nDesign: Increased Perception +4 (B16, 5/level, 20 points): a broad \"notices everything\" bonus, deliberately not split into Astartes- style Acute Vision/Hearing/Smell packages since the fiction doesn't describe Custodian senses that granularly.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Imperium",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 4
				},
				{
					"id": "Tjrn3FF7n7qYR5pb4",
					"name": "Custodian Regeneration",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Custodians shrug off and recover from wounds that would permanently cripple or kill an Astartes, a detail repeated across Wraight's novels (Custodians returning to duty from injuries a Space Marine would need months of Apothecarion care to survive). This project reads that as healing markedly faster than the Astartes package's Larraman's-Organ-based Rapid Healing, not as literal invulnerability.\n\nDesign: Regeneration (Slow, B80, 10 points) for passive healing even without rest, plus Very Rapid Healing (B80, 15 points, quadruple effective HT for natural recovery) for a step above the Astartes' plain Rapid Healing (double effective HT). 25 points total.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Imperium",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"container_type": "meta_trait",
					"children": [
						{
							"id": "t8qOMLXJxRnJSf-MS",
							"name": "Regeneration (Slow)",
							"reference": "B80",
							"local_notes": "Recovers 1 HP per 12 hours even without rest or first aid.",
							"base_points": 10
						},
						{
							"id": "tvWzSsjU6mRF3RMn6",
							"name": "Very Rapid Healing",
							"reference": "B80",
							"base_points": 15
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "T596om_uSYLEnCTHU",
					"name": "Custodian Constitution",
					"local_notes": "Lore: No single source itemises separate anti-toxin organs for Custodians the way Codex Astartes background does for the Preomnor and Oolitic Kidney; what the fiction consistently shows is a Custodian simply shrugging off poisons, plagues, and pain that would down anyone else outright.\n\nDesign: Resistant (B81), Very Common: Metabolic Hazards (disease, poison, toxins, radiation sickness and the like), Immunity tier - base 30, x1 for Immunity = 30 points, deliberately a single unified trait rather than the Astartes package's several organ-specific ones (Preomnor 10 + Oolitic Kidney 15 + part of Melanchromic Organ 11 = 36 split across three separate hazards): a Custodian's constitution doesn't have gaps between what different organs happen to cover. High Pain Threshold (B59, 10 points) as the Astartes package. 40 points total.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Imperium",
						"Adeptus Custodes"
					],
					"container_type": "meta_trait",
					"children": [
						{
							"id": "tDhTUIMMa5w5EpTfu",
							"name": "Resistant to Disease and Toxins",
							"reference": "B81",
							"local_notes": "Resistant (Very Common: Disease, Poison, Toxins, etc.), Immunity.",
							"base_points": 30
						},
						{
							"id": "tsNPUVJBPLwhJutqU",
							"name": "High Pain Threshold",
							"reference": "B59",
							"base_points": 10
						}
					]
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TqZQynELfQR_b3V77",
			"name": "Custodian Oath and Mindset",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Custodians are famously remote even from other Imperial elites - Inquisitors, Chapter Masters and High Lords alike are received with cool, absolute courtesy rather than camaraderie - and Wraight's novels repeatedly frame this as focus rather than arrogance: nothing matters to a Custodian except the duty in front of him. The historic vow keeping the Legio on Terra is well documented in current Codex background and its lifting during the Indomitus Crusade is likewise current-edition canon, not a house rule.\n\nDesign: Duty (-15) and Sense of Duty (-5) mirror the Astartes package's structure but narrow the in-group to the Legio Custodes itself, per the \"aloof even from fellow elites\" note. Code of Honor (-10) is written from scratch rather than reusing Codex Astartes' text, since a Custodian answers to no Chapter. The Vow is included but disabled by default for the reason given above; enabling it is a straight -10 swap, nothing else in this file needs to change. Aloof (-1 Quirk) is the cheapest possible flag for the \"doesn't warm to outsiders\" personality note repeated across the fiction, kept small because nothing in the sources supports a harsher social disadvantage (Custodians aren't shown as cruel or friendless, just focused). Net: -15-5-10-1 = -31 with the Vow disabled, -41 enabled.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Custodes"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tuLJnlRB_9OaV8kIF",
					"name": "Duty (The Emperor and the Throneworld)",
					"reference": "B133",
					"local_notes": "Almost all the time, Extremely Hazardous.",
					"base_points": -15
				},
				{
					"id": "tRomLn6C6bHxKBS6w",
					"name": "Sense of Duty (The Ten Thousand)",
					"reference": "B153",
					"local_notes": "Small group: fellow Custodians and the Throne they guard, not the wider Imperium.",
					"base_points": -5
				},
				{
					"id": "tdWoj5MflnhtnjLMz",
					"name": "Code of Honor (Custodian's Creed)",
					"reference": "B127",
					"local_notes": "Never leave a post while the Emperor lives; never speak falsely to the Throne; accept correction from no authority outside the Legio Custodes and the Emperor himself.",
					"base_points": -10
				},
				{
					"id": "t8tjTrNMT0DxBCqQj",
					"name": "Vow (Never Leave the Emperor's Side / Terra)",
					"reference": "B161",
					"local_notes": "Major Vow. Disabled by default: this project follows current Codex background and the Indomitus Crusade-era novels, where Guilliman as Lord Commander formally released Custodians for wider deployment after ten thousand years of confinement to Terra. Enable this for a Custodian bound by the older, absolute reading of the vow (pre-Indomitus, or a GM who wants the classic \"the Ten Thousand never leave Terra\" status quo).",
					"base_points": -10,
					"disabled": true
				},
				{
					"id": "t1JZsgc9Y3YhUwnHk",
					"name": "Aloof",
					"local_notes": "Rarely engages with anyone outside the Legio Custodes as an equal, the Emperor excepted.",
					"tags": [
						"Quirk"
					],
					"base_points": -1
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tclgclRVernYJL5W0",
			"name": "Rank (Shield-Captain, Legio Custodes)",
			"reference": "B29",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Shield-Captain commands a Custodian Guard shield-host and answers only to the Captain-General and the Emperor himself; current Codex background and Wraight's novels (which feature named Shield-Captains prominently) place the role above even a Chapter Master in Imperial precedence when Throneworld security is at stake.\n\nDesign: Rank (B29), 5 points/level, 9 levels = 45 points: three levels above the rank-and-file Custodian Guardian's own Rank 6 (30 points), reflecting genuine command authority over other Custodians rather than simple elite standing.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Custodes"
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 9
		},
		{
			"id": "tlbd4HZCw1_4S8bg9",
			"name": "Reputation (A Shield-Captain of the Ten Thousand)",
			"reference": "B27",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Shield-Captain's heraldry marks him as one of the handful of Custodians trusted with independent command; background shows even Inquisitors and High Lords deferring further to a Shield-Captain than to an ordinary Custodian Guard.\n\nDesign: Reputation (B27), priced above the Guardian template's own Reputation (10 points for +3) at 15 points for a +4 reaction, following the same eyeballed-against-precedent approach used throughout this project's Reputation traits.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Custodes"
			],
			"base_points": 15,
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "reaction_bonus",
					"situation": "from anyone in the Imperium who recognizes a Shield-Captain's rank markings",
					"amount": 4
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tz_Q_aFVzM-krIy7Y",
			"name": "Personal Advancement",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Custodian raised to independent command has, per the fiction, put in centuries more service than a line Guardian even before taking on a shield-host, on top of the personal qualities that got him noticed for the role in the first place.\n\nDesign: DX+2 (40) + IQ+1 (20) + HT+1 (10) + Will+2 (10) + Per+2 (10) = 90, rounded up to a flat 140-point package for the same \"lump-sum veteran seasoning\" reason the Guardian template's own Personal Advancement trait uses, scaled up for a Shield-Captain's greater seniority.",
			"tags": [
				"Physical",
				"Mental"
			],
			"base_points": 140,
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "attribute_bonus",
					"attribute": "dx",
					"amount": 2
				},
				{
					"type": "attribute_bonus",
					"attribute": "iq",
					"amount": 1
				},
				{
					"type": "attribute_bonus",
					"attribute": "ht",
					"amount": 1
				},
				{
					"type": "attribute_bonus",
					"attribute": "will",
					"amount": 2
				},
				{
					"type": "attribute_bonus",
					"attribute": "per",
					"amount": 2
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tzrXY_kUVJ10Jyt9y",
			"name": "Weapon Master (Custodes Power Weapons)",
			"reference": "B43",
			"local_notes": "Lore: See the Custodian Guardian template; a Shield-Captain has this at least as thoroughly as any Guardian under his command.\n\nDesign: Weapon Master (B43), small class (the Custodes power-weapon family), 30 points, as the Guardian template.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical"
			],
			"base_points": 30
		},
		{
			"id": "t7FBKuqs9eiZ2cDOo",
			"name": "Ambidexterity",
			"reference": "B39",
			"local_notes": "Lore: As the Guardian template.\n\nDesign: Ambidexterity (B39, 5 points), as the Guardian template.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical"
			],
			"base_points": 5
		},
		{
			"id": "tqI9KMRxAM9jJsuyW",
			"name": "Trained by a Master",
			"reference": "B90",
			"local_notes": "Lore: As the Guardian template, and more so for a Shield-Captain's greater seniority.\n\nDesign: Trained by a Master (B90, 30 points), as the Guardian template.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical"
			],
			"base_points": 30
		},
		{
			"id": "tYmbwERaF9fkAAW3C",
			"name": "Charisma",
			"reference": "B41",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Shield-Captain leads other Custodians - each individually a match for a Chapter Master - by presence and proven judgement rather than by threat or bluster; Wraight's novels consistently show named Shield-Captains commanding instant, unforced deference from their own Custodians.\n\nDesign: Charisma 3 (B41, 5/level, 15 points): a reaction and command-skill bonus reflecting an officer's presence, not itself a leadership doctrine (that's the skills below).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social"
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 3
		},
		{
			"id": "tmv897pHKH0YBdQn9",
			"name": "Tactical Command Presence",
			"reference": "B133",
			"local_notes": "Duty (own shield-host's survival), Almost all the time, Extremely Hazardous, stacking with the wider Custodian Duty.\n\nLore: A Shield-Captain answers for every Custodian under his command in a way a line Guardian does not; Codex background frames losing Custodians under one's own command as a Shield-Captain's personal failure, not merely a Legio-wide loss.\n\nDesign: A second, narrower Duty (B133, -10) layered on top of the Custodian Oath and Mindset package's own Duty (to the Emperor and Throneworld): command responsibility for a specific shield-host is an additional, personal burden a line Guardian doesn't carry.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental"
			],
			"base_points": -10
		}
	],
